key_passage.text:
Polynomial regression is just like linear regression except that the hypothesis space is polynomial functions rather than linear functions, that is, $$\begin{aligned}
y = f_{\theta}(x) = \sum_{k=0}^K \theta_k x^k
\end{aligned}$$ where $K$, the degree of the polynomial, is a hyperparameter of the hypothesis space.

concepts.content:
The general form of a regularized objective function is:
$$\begin{aligned}
    J(\theta) = \overbrace{\frac{1}{N} \sum^N_{i=1} \mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(x)^{(i)}, y^{(i)})}^\text{data fit loss} + \underbrace{\lambda R(\theta)}_\text{regularizer} \quad\quad\triangleleft \quad\text{regularized objective function}
\end{aligned}$$

